these farmers in lebanon are harvesting  lettuce meant for households in saudi  arabia  but after the saudi government imposed  the ban on lebanese produce  they fear the agricultural sector will  face a disaster  saudi officials say the shipments are  being used to smuggle drugs  it's an arbitrary decision so we ask the  saudis to reverse the ban  we're not all drug dealers and smugglers  you can't punish everyone  lebanon exports four hundred thousand  tons of agricultural produce every year  one-fourth to saudi arabia and the wider  gulf region  it's millions of dollars for a country  facing a deep economic  and financial crisis manor men and  we don't export pomegranates  investigations show the truck came from  syria  but yes you can argue the lebanese  government is not doing its job there  are no scanners at the borders with  syria or at the ports  corruption in governance has plagued  this country for years  there is little control along its  borders whether with neighboring syria  or at ports where smuggling is rampant  and evasion of custom tax costs the  state  billions of dollars in revenues  lebanon has a long history of drug  cultivation and trafficking  the bika valley is a source of opium and  hashish  production factories producing captagon  pills  have been discovered as well western  drug agencies say  the amphetamine is also widely  manufactured in syria  the discovery of 5.3 million captagon  pills in saudi arabia  was the latest drug bust coming from  lebanon the timing of the ban  has raised the question if politics is  at play  this disengagement strategy that saudi  uh  arabia as well as the broader gulf has  adopted vis-a-vis lebanon  is slowly turning into more of an  engagement but a negative engagement  strategy in that case to really up the  pressure on the lebanese government  saudi arabia whose influence in lebanon  has waned in recent years  is angered by what many see is the  iranian-backed hezbollah's control  and influence in the country lebanon's  leaders  ordered the security services to crack  down on drug smuggling and those behind  it  decisions are not in the hands of the  lebanese government but in the hands of  hezbollah the smuggling routes are under  its control  so is the airport and the ports there is  no state  in lebanon there is a lot of uncertainty  saudi arabia wants guarantees that what  it calls  systematic smuggling operations and few  here believe  it will be easy to control the drug  trade senator al jazeera beirut
 

